{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"b4k4p0sks","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XmYK7VXyUWPW1t9nHrSbhf","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-441489/-scratch-users-kushinm-auto-psych-outer-loop-live-runs-run3-repo/f82f69d3-ad8e-4273-a544-0aa56d44ef83/tasks/b4k4p0sks.output","summary":"Background command \"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_3\nM=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/models/streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip.py\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md << 'EOF'\nRefinement of the incumbent `streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip` (people judge a sequence random to the extent their own second-order, streak-weary picture of a fair coin — one that expects a long run to break — explains it better than the regular generators they suspect: a switch-biased coin, a heads-favoured trick coin and a repeating motif with slips, with pseudo-flip-diluted per-flip evidence, heavy-tailed signed person sensitivity and a side habit). The single change is in how lopsided people imagine a trick coin to be: instead of a fixed, flat spread of possible rigging strengths, the spread is fitted, so people may picture trick coins as only mildly rigged (or as grossly rigged), which sets how much a modest heads/tails imbalance counts as evidence of a trick coin relative to switching. This addresses the critique that among length-8 pairs where the more-switching sequence is also the more lopsided, people choose the more-switching sequence more often than the incumbent predicts — they weigh heads/tails balance against switching less than its flat trick-coin prior implies.\nEOF\necho trick_coin_spread_streak_weary > $D/model_name.txt\npython3 - \"$M\" \"$D/candidate.py\" << 'PY'\nimport sys\nsrc=open(sys.argv[1]).read()\nhead='''\"\"\"Fitted rigging spread of the trick coin in the streak-weary pseudo-flip judgment.\n\nRefinement of the incumbent `streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip` (described below).\nSingle change: the trick coin's prior over its heads rate is Beta(kappa s,\nkappa (1 - s)) with the concentration kappa fitted (kappa = 2 recovers the\nincumbent's Beta(2 s, 2 (1 - s))). Large kappa: people picture trick coins as\nonly mildly rigged, so a modest heads/tails imbalance is weak evidence against\nchance; small kappa: they picture grossly rigged coins. Addresses the critique\nthat among length-8 pairs where the more-switching sequence is the more\nlopsided, people choose the more-switching sequence more often than predicted\n(balance weighed less against switching than the flat prior implies).\n\nDescription of the refined model follows.\n\n'''\nsrc=src.replace('\"\"\"Gambler\\'s run-length belief', head+\"Gambler's run-length belief\",1)\nold='''    a_h = 2.0 * s_heads\n    a_t = 2.0 - a_h\n    seq_lbias = (\n        pt.gammaln(seq_heads + a_h) + pt.gammaln(seq_len - seq_heads + a_t)\n        - pt.gammaln(seq_len + 2.0)\n        - pt.gammaln(a_h) - pt.gammaln(a_t) + math.lgamma(2.0)\n    )'''\nnew='''    # Fitted concentration of the rigging prior (kappa = 2: the incumbent's).\n    log_kappa = pm.Normal(\"log_kappa\", mu=math.log(2.0), sigma=1.0)\n    kappa = pm.Deterministic(\"kappa\", pt.exp(log_kappa))\n    a_h = kappa * s_heads\n    a_t = kappa - a_h\n    seq_lbias = (\n        pt.gammaln(seq_heads + a_h) + pt.gammaln(seq_len - seq_heads + a_t)\n        - pt.gammaln(seq_len + kappa)\n        - pt.gammaln(a_h) - pt.gammaln(a_t) + pt.gammaln(kappa)\n    )'''\nassert old in src\nsrc=src.replace(old,new)\nopen(sys.argv[2],'w').write(src)\nPY\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && timeout 1500 /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir $D --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5\" completed (exit code 0)","uuid":"cd83abfd-27dd-4cd9-8bd2-4809f837769a","session_id":"f82f69d3-ad8e-4273-a544-0aa56d44ef83"}
